.* XLSX01H - help panel group of command XLSX01 (Import Excel XLSX to table)
.* Build: CPYFRMSTMF to QTEMP/QPNLSRC(XLSX01H), CRTPNLGRP, CRTCMD HLPPNLGRP
.* (see XLSXDIST.clle).  Lines max 80 columns.  Version 1.4.1 (2026-11-17)
:PNLGRP.
:HELP NAME='XLSX01'.Import Excel XLSX to table - Help
:P.The Import Excel XLSX to table (XLSX01) command reads the rows of a
worksheet of a Microsoft Excel workbook (.xlsx, Office Open XML) from a
stream file of the integrated file system and inserts them into a Db2
table. The table can be created by the command or can already exist.
:P.The workbook is read by an ILE RPG program (XLSX10R) without Java, PASE,
ODBC or a PC. Workbooks written by Excel, LibreOffice, Google Sheets,
openpyxl, XlsxWriter and the XLSX00 command can be read.
:P.With the default values the command finds the data by itself&colon.
:UL.
:LI.
:P.The first Excel table of the sheet, or the range of an active
autofilter, is used when there is one.
:LI.
:P.Otherwise title rows above the data (rows with few filled cells) are
skipped and the data ends at the last filled row. Empty rows inside the
data are ignored.
:LI.
:P.The first row is used as header when it holds only distinct texts over
numbers or dates, or texts that do not repeat in the data.
:LI.
:P.When the table is created, the type of each column is chosen from all
the values of the column&colon. INTEGER, BIGINT, DECIMAL, DOUBLE, DATE,
TIME, TIMESTAMP, SMALLINT for TRUE/FALSE, and VARCHAR for text. Column
names come from the headings (or COL_A, COL_B...), with the heading as
column text and column heading.
:LI.
:P.When the table exists, the Excel columns are matched to the table
columns by heading (column name, system name, text or column heading) or
else by position, and every value is converted to the column type.
:EUL.
:P.With ERRORS(*STOP), the default, all values are checked before the
first row is inserted&colon. if one does not fit, nothing is written and
the messages tell the cell, the value and the reason.
:P.Version 1.4.1.
:P.:HP2.Restrictions&colon.:EHP2.
:UL.
:LI.
:P.At most 1000 columns are read. Formulas are read as their last
calculated value, which Excel stores in the file. Cells with an Excel
error (#N/A, #DIV/0! ...) are read as empty cells.
:LI.
:P.The .xlsb, .xls and .ods formats and password protected (encrypted)
workbooks are not supported; save them as .xlsx first.
:LI.
:P.The rows are inserted without commitment control, in blocks of up to
1000 rows. With ERRORS(*SKIP) or ERRORS(*NULL) the rows inserted before
an unexpected database error stay in the table.
:LI.
:P.Text that is longer than 32,740 bytes in UTF-8 is cut.
:EUL.
:EHELP.
.* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
:HELP NAME='XLSX01/FROMSTMF'.From stream file (FROMSTMF) - Help
:XH3.From stream file (FROMSTMF)
:P.Specifies the path of the workbook, for example /home/user/orders.xlsx.
This is a required parameter.
:EHELP.
.* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
:HELP NAME='XLSX01/TOFILE'.To table (TOFILE) - Help
:XH3.To table (TOFILE)
:P.Specifies the table that receives the rows. This is a required
parameter.
:PARML.
:PT.:PV.table-name:EPV.
:PD.The system name of the table or physical file. When it does not exist
and CRTFILE(*YES) is specified, it is created.
:PT.*NONE
:PD.Nothing is written to the database. The workbook is analysed and the
result (sheets, data area, header, column types and the CREATE TABLE
statement that would be used) is written to the REPORT stream file and
to the job log.
:EPARML.
:P.The possible library values are&colon.
:PARML.
:PT.:PK DEF.*LIBL:EPK.
:PD.The library list is searched; a new table is created in the current
library.
:PT.*CURLIB
:PD.The current library of the job.
:PT.:PV.library-name:EPV.
:PD.The library of the table.
:EPARML.
:EHELP.
.* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
:HELP NAME='XLSX01/SHEET'.Sheet name or number (SHEET) - Help
:XH3.Sheet name or number (SHEET)
:P.Specifies the worksheet to read.
:PARML.
:PT.:PK DEF.*FIRST:EPK.
:PD.The first visible worksheet of the workbook.
:PT.:PV.sheet-name:EPV.
:PD.The name of the sheet, as shown on its tab. Uppercase and lowercase
letters are not distinguished.
:PT.:PV.sheet-number:EPV.
:PD.The position of the sheet in the workbook, 1 for the first one,
hidden sheets included.
:EPARML.
:EHELP.
.* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
:HELP NAME='XLSX01/RANGE'.Range (RANGE) - Help
:XH3.Cell range, table or name (RANGE)
:P.Specifies the cells to read.
:PARML.
:PT.:PK DEF.*AUTO:EPK.
:PD.The first Excel table of the sheet, else the range of the autofilter
of the sheet (with the rows added below it), else the filled cells found
by the heuristic described in the command help.
:PT.:PV.cell-range:EPV.
:PD.A range like B4&colon.H200, whole columns like B&colon.H, or the top
left cell like B4 (the data then goes on to the last filled row and
column). The first row of the range is the header row when there is one.
:PT.:PV.table-name:EPV.
:PD.The name of an Excel table (Insert > Table) of any sheet. Its header
row and totals row are handled automatically.
:PT.:PV.defined-name:EPV.
:PD.A workbook defined name (Formulas > Name Manager) that refers to a cell
range.
:EPARML.
:EHELP.
.* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
:HELP NAME='XLSX01/HEADER'.Header row (HEADER) - Help
:XH3.Header row (HEADER)
:P.Specifies whether the first row of the data area holds column headings.
:PARML.
:PT.:PK DEF.*AUTO:EPK.
:PD.Decided from the cells&colon. a row of distinct texts over numbers,
dates or texts that differ from it is a header row.
:PT.*YES
:PD.The first row holds the headings. They are used to name new columns
and to match existing columns.
:PT.*NO
:PD.The first row is data. New columns are named COL_A, COL_B ... after
the Excel column letters, and existing columns are matched by position.
:PT.*SKIP
:PD.The first row is a header, but it is not used&colon. the row is
skipped and columns are named and matched as with *NO.
:EPARML.
:EHELP.
.* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
:HELP NAME='XLSX01/STARTROW'.First row (STARTROW) - Help
:XH3.First row (STARTROW)
:P.Specifies the sheet row where the data area starts (the header row
when there is one). Rows above it are ignored, for example report titles.
:PARML.
:PT.:PK DEF.*AUTO:EPK.
:PD.The first row of RANGE, or the first row found by the heuristic.
:PT.:PV.row-number:EPV.
:PD.A row number from 1 to 1048576, as shown by Excel.
:EPARML.
:EHELP.
.* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
:HELP NAME='XLSX01/ROWSEL'.Rows to read (ROWSEL) - Help
:XH3.Rows to read (ROWSEL)
:PARML.
:PT.:PK DEF.*ALL:EPK.
:PD.All the rows of the data area are read.
:PT.*VISIBLE
:PD.Rows hidden by an Excel filter, or hidden by hand, are skipped. Use it
to import only what the user filtered in Excel. The filter must have been
applied by Excel before the file was saved.
:EPARML.
:EHELP.
.* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
:HELP NAME='XLSX01/MAXROWS'.Maximum rows (MAXROWS) - Help
:XH3.Maximum rows (MAXROWS)
:P.Specifies the maximum number of data rows to insert.
:PARML.
:PT.:PK DEF.*ALL:EPK.
:PD.All the rows are inserted.
:PT.:PV.number:EPV.
:PD.Only the first rows are inserted, for example to try a big file.
:EPARML.
:EHELP.
.* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
:HELP NAME='XLSX01/MBROPT'.Replace or add rows (MBROPT) - Help
:XH3.Replace or add rows (MBROPT)
:PARML.
:PT.:PK DEF.*ADD:EPK.
:PD.The rows are added to the rows already in the table.
:PT.*REPLACE
:PD.The rows of an existing table are deleted (TRUNCATE, or DELETE when
truncate is not allowed) after all the values have been checked and
before the new rows are inserted.
:EPARML.
:EHELP.
.* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
:HELP NAME='XLSX01/CRTFILE'.Create table (CRTFILE) - Help
:XH3.Create table (CRTFILE)
:PARML.
:PT.:PK DEF.*YES:EPK.
:PD.A table that does not exist is created with the columns and types
found in the sheet. An existing table is used as it is.
:PT.*NO
:PD.The table must exist.
:PT.*REPLACE
:PD.An existing table is dropped and created again from the sheet. Use it
for work tables only&colon. indexes, views, authorities and triggers over
the old table are lost.
:EPARML.
:EHELP.
.* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
:HELP NAME='XLSX01/MAP'.Column mapping (MAP) - Help
:XH3.Column mapping (MAP)
:P.Specifies how the Excel columns are matched to the columns of an
existing table.
:PARML.
:PT.:PK DEF.*AUTO:EPK.
:PD.By heading when at least one heading matches a column, else by
position.
:PT.*NAME
:PD.By heading only. A heading matches the column name, the system column
name, the column text or the column heading, ignoring case, blanks and
punctuation. Excel columns without a match are ignored and reported.
:PT.*POS
:PD.The first Excel column goes to the first table column and so on.
Identity columns and columns generated always are skipped.
:EPARML.
:P.Table columns without an Excel column receive their default value. A
NOT NULL column without default that gets no value stops the command.
:EHELP.
.* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
:HELP NAME='XLSX01/COLMAP'.Column map (COLMAP) - Help
:XH3.Column map (COLMAP)
:P.Specifies explicit pairs Excel column = table column, separated by
semicolons. The Excel column is given by its heading or by its letter.
*SKIP as table column ignores the Excel column. Pairs not given follow
the MAP parameter.
:XMP.
COLMAP('Customer no.=CUSNUM; Amount EUR=AMOUNT; F=*SKIP')
:EXMP.
:P.For a new table, COLMAP gives the names of the new columns.
:EHELP.
.* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
:HELP NAME='XLSX01/TYPES'.Column types (TYPES) - Help
:XH3.Column types (TYPES)
:P.Specifies the SQL type of columns of a new table, instead of the type
found from the values. Pairs column=type are separated by semicolons; the
column is given by its new name, its heading or its Excel letter.
:XMP.
TYPES('ZIP=CHAR(5); AMOUNT=DECIMAL(11,2); C=VARCHAR(200)')
:EXMP.
:P.With TYPES the values are checked like for an existing table.
:EHELP.
.* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
:HELP NAME='XLSX01/NULLS'.Nullable new columns (NULLS) - Help
:XH3.Nullable new columns (NULLS)
:PARML.
:PT.:PK DEF.*NO:EPK.
:PD.New columns are NOT NULL WITH DEFAULT&colon. empty cells become
blanks, zero, 0001-01-01 for dates or 00.00.00 for times.
:PT.*YES
:PD.New columns allow null values&colon. empty cells become NULL.
:EPARML.
:EHELP.
.* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
:HELP NAME='XLSX01/ERRORS'.Errors (ERRORS) - Help
:XH3.Values that do not fit (ERRORS)
:P.Specifies what happens with a value that cannot be converted to its
column, for example a text in a numeric column, a number with too many
digits or a text that is too long.
:PARML.
:PT.:PK DEF.*STOP:EPK.
:PD.All the values are checked before anything is written. When one value
does not fit, no row is inserted, a new table is dropped, and the first
20 errors are sent as diagnostic messages.
:PT.*SKIP
:PD.Rows with a value that does not fit are not inserted. The other rows
are.
:PT.*NULL
:PD.A value that does not fit becomes NULL, or the default value when the
column does not allow null values. Text that is too long is cut.
:EPARML.
:EHELP.
.* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
:HELP NAME='XLSX01/STRCCSID'.Text CCSID (STRCCSID) - Help
:XH3.CCSID of new text columns (STRCCSID)
:PARML.
:PT.:PK DEF.*AUTO:EPK.
:PD.Text columns get the CCSID of the job, unless a value has characters
the job CCSID cannot hold (Greek, Cyrillic, Chinese ...)&colon. then the
column is VARCHAR CCSID 1208 (UTF-8).
:PT.*JOB
:PD.The CCSID of the job for all text columns. Characters that it cannot
hold are replaced by the substitution character.
:PT.*UTF8
:PD.VARCHAR CCSID 1208 for all text columns.
:PT.*UTF16
:PD.VARGRAPHIC CCSID 1200 for all text columns.
:PT.:PV.ccsid:EPV.
:PD.A single-byte CCSID such as 37, 280 or 1144.
:EPARML.
:EHELP.
.* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
:HELP NAME='XLSX01/TRIM'.Trim blanks of text (TRIM) - Help
:XH3.Trim blanks of text (TRIM)
:PARML.
:PT.:PK DEF.*YES:EPK.
:PD.Leading and trailing blanks of text cells are removed.
:PT.*NO
:PD.Texts are kept as they are.
:EPARML.
:EHELP.
.* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
:HELP NAME='XLSX01/DATFMT'.Order of text dates (DATFMT) - Help
:XH3.Order of text dates (DATFMT)
:P.Specifies how dates written as text, like 03/04/2026, are read. Real
Excel dates and ISO texts like 2026-04-03 are always read correctly.
:PARML.
:PT.:PK DEF.*DMY:EPK.
:PD.Day, month, year (03/04/2026 is 3 April).
:PT.*MDY
:PD.Month, day, year (03/04/2026 is 4 March).
:PT.*YMD
:PD.Year, month, day.
:EPARML.
:EHELP.
.* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
:HELP NAME='XLSX01/REPORT'.Report stream file (REPORT) - Help
:XH3.Report stream file (REPORT)
:P.Specifies a stream file that receives a UTF-8 text report&colon.
sheets of the workbook, data area and how it was found, header decision,
each column with its type, mapping and statistics, the SQL statements,
errors and timings.
:PARML.
:PT.:PK DEF.*NONE:EPK.
:PD.No report is written.
:PT.:PV.path-name:EPV.
:PD.The path of the report; an existing file is replaced.
:EPARML.
:EHELP.
.* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
:HELP NAME='XLSX01/COMMAND/EXAMPLES'.Examples for XLSX01 - Help
:XH3.Examples for XLSX01
:P.:HP2.Example 1&colon. Create a table from a workbook:EHP2.
:XMP.
XLSX01 FROMSTMF('/home/user/prices.xlsx') TOFILE(MYLIB/PRICES)
:EXMP.
:P.Creates table PRICES from the first sheet, with names and types found
from the data.
:P.:HP2.Example 2&colon. Refresh an existing table:EHP2.
:XMP.
XLSX01 FROMSTMF('/home/user/rates.xlsx') TOFILE(MYLIB/RATES)
       SHEET('2026') MBROPT(*REPLACE) CRTFILE(*NO)
:EXMP.
:P.Checks all the values of sheet 2026, then replaces the rows of RATES.
:P.:HP2.Example 3&colon. Only the filtered rows of a report:EHP2.
:XMP.
XLSX01 FROMSTMF('/home/user/open.xlsx') TOFILE(QTEMP/OPEN)
       STARTROW(4) ROWSEL(*VISIBLE)
:EXMP.
:P.:HP2.Example 4&colon. Analyse only:EHP2.
:XMP.
XLSX01 FROMSTMF('/home/user/x.xlsx') TOFILE(*NONE)
       REPORT('/home/user/x.txt')
:EXMP.
:P.:HP2.Example 5&colon. Call from RPG:EHP2.
:XMP.
/copy '/home/user/xlsx/XLSX10PR.rpgleinc'
XLSX10R('/home/user/x.xlsx' : 'ORDERS    MYLIB     ' : rows : msg
       : 'Data' : *omit : '*YES');
:EXMP.
:P.With the parameter msg, errors are returned in msg (blank when the
import succeeded) instead of an escape message; rows receives the number
of rows inserted.
:EHELP.
.* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
:HELP NAME='XLSX01/ERROR/MESSAGES'.Error messages - Help
:XH3.Error messages for XLSX01
:P.:HP3.*ESCAPE messages:EHP3.
:DL COMPACT.
:DT.CPF9898
:DD.The import failed. The text starts with XLSX10R and tells the reason,
for example a file that is not a workbook, a sheet that does not exist,
values that do not fit (see the diagnostic messages before it) or an SQL
error with its SQLSTATE.
:EDL.
:P.:HP3.*DIAG messages:EHP3.
:DL COMPACT.
:DT.CPF9897
:DD.One message for each of the first 20 values that do not fit&colon.
cell, value, column and reason.
:EDL.
:P.:HP3.*COMP messages:EHP3.
:DL COMPACT.
:DT.CPF9897
:DD.Rows inserted, sheet, table and elapsed seconds.
:EDL.
:EHELP.
:EPNLGRP.
